# ~/.claude config repo — ALLOWLIST gitignore.
# Ignore EVERYTHING by default, then opt in only the config I actually maintain. This is the safe
# pattern for a repo that gets pushed: a new secret/runtime file added later can't leak in unless I
# explicitly track it. See README.md for the full inventory + reproduce-on-a-new-machine steps.

# 1) Ignore all top-level entries by default.
/*

# 2) Opt in the config.
!/.gitignore
!/LICENSE
!/README.md
!/AGENTS.md
!/CLAUDE.md
!/settings.json
!/identity.example.json
!/hooks/
!/.githooks/
!/dotfiles/
!/commands/
!/references/
!/rules/
!/bin/
!/connectors/
# Real connector manifests are a PER-USER untracked overlay (like identity.local.json).
# Only the *.example templates are tracked.
/connectors/*.json
!/contracts/
!/console/
!/security-guard-allowlist.txt

# 3) Inside skills/, track ONLY your own personal plugin. Everything else there is third-party
#    (gstack, impeccable, agency-agents, …), an embedded git repo, or a symlink.
#    Your work-skills plugin (e.g. sk-work) is deliberately NOT opted back in: it is job-specific,
#    lives in /work/ (also covered by the default-ignore at the top), and reaches Claude Code through
#    a symlink here. Untracked by omission from this allowlist rather than by an ignore rule.
!/skills/
/skills/*
!/skills/sk/

# 4) Belt-and-suspenders: never commit a secret or runtime artifact, even inside a tracked dir.
.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.key
**/*.pem
**/*.p12
**/*.pfx
**/id_rsa
**/id_dsa
**/id_ecdsa
**/id_ed25519
**/auth.json
**/.credentials.json
**/*credentials*.json
**/*.token
**/*.secret
**/*.log
**/node_modules/
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
**/.pytest_cache/
